WEBVTT

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.400
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department, Cal State Long Beach. So we want to illustrate

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:13.220
the other type of FSM, which is Mealy type of FSM. And here the function h is defined

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:19.500
such that combination of state and input are going to produce the output. So output will

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:29.580
depend on both the state and the input value. So let us do a couple of very quick examples.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:39.260
So first, let's say we have initial state as a 0, and in that initial state as a 0, we're

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:49.580
going to say on state 0, on input equal to 0, we produce output equals to 0.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:01:00.980
So this is output and this is input.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:13.920
On input 1, we are going to produce 0 still, but we are going to transition to state S1.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:21.280
On state S1, on output 0, we are producing 1, sorry, on input 0, we are producing 1,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:28.060
and on input 1, we are also producing 1, but going back to state S0.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:30.660
That is as close as possible.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.700
It can be to functionality that we have previous.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:39.180
Please bear in mind that the way that Miele FSM works

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.020
is slightly different with respect to time.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:47.860
And I'm referring you to a simulation in the book.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:53.300
I also want to point out a different example.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:58.930
So this is example Miele.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:28.260
example number two, which kind of has very similar look to it. So here when we are in state as zero,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:38.180
if input is zero, we are producing zero and we are staying in state zero. When we are in state zero

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:49.380
and input is 1. We will be producing 1, but then transitioning to state S1. When

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:57.860
transitioning from state 1, this is why we have two different edges. The reason

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:06.220
for that is that on input 0, the output is going to be 1. On input 1, the

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.420
the output is going to be zero.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:13.420
This is different and new from the previous example.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:18.060
Another thing that we need to take care of,

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.540
and I will be repeating that all over and over again,

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.740
if you have a state,

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.860
and states do not need to be called S0, S1, S2, S3.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:33.860
We can have a state that is called call.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:45.140
or something like that. If you have a state, and from that state, well, sorry, if you

00:03:45.140 --> 00:04:00.700
have state and we have total of one input variable, because our input

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:07.740
variables are binary, we are going to have total 2 to the number of 1, which

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.220
which is total two different possibilities.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:16.220
Make sure that you have two outgoing edges.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.380
It doesn't matter kind of when they,

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:23.100
I mean, it matters for the purpose of functionality,

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.060
but for the purpose of your self-checking,

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:29.300
one of those can go back to the same state.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:30.660
But you have to make sure

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:35.240
that you have two outgoing edges.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:39.960
If number of input variables is three,

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.480
you are going to have two to the three,

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.960
which is eight outgoing edges.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:47.240
Please count those edges,

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:52.240
that is first way for you to figure out

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:57.240
and check your work when you're designing an FSM.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.680
That's an easy way to check,

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.900
but the meaning behind this is such

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:14.820
that in each state you need to define, for all inputs, how do we transition?

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:15.620
Thanks for watching.

